00:00:01.969 --> 00:00:03.860 align:start position:0%
so we have the latest jobs numbers and  once again the latest jobs report has  missed expectations but I want to talk  about it specifically in order really to  have a conversation I want to make more  of an effort to go beyond just the  latest numbers and to really make sure  we're understanding the big issues that  are underlying these jobs numbers and  the systemic economic problems that are  going to have to be solved if we're  really going to progress as a society  and progress as an economy for the sort  of betterment and well-being of  everybody in the country on the planet  whatever so as a starting point let's  quickly deal with the knob and the  numbers themselves new jobs again have  slowed down they fell short of expected  numbers in May only seventy five  thousand jobs were added in May the  expected number was 175,000 so number  one it's really important to understand  that because more and more people live  in the United States over time and based  on their ages the number of working age  people also fluctuates but as a really  general idea you need to create some new  jobs just to keep up with population  growth there are different estimates of  what that number is I've seen as low as  80,000 jobs a month I've seen as high as  a hundred and twenty thousand jobs a  month it's of complex and pretty widely  contested number and I know no matter  what number arrange I give some people  are going to disagree with me but  understand the concept  if 75,000 new jobs were created and you  need 80 thousand or a hundred thousand  or 120 thousand just to account for  population growth we're falling behind  one way or the other the only debate is  how much are we falling behind when we  only create 75,000 jobs now the trend  line is also a concern because we got  below expected numbers for several  months in a row the numbers from March  were revised down from the initial  reports March was initially reported at  189 thousand and then the numbers were  fine-tuned and we found out it was  actually a hundred and fifty three  thousand new jobs in March which isn't  great April was reported as two hundred  and sixty three thousand it was  fine-tuned and then it turns out it was  actually only two hundred and twenty  four thousand new job  so unemployment is at a historically low  rate new job numbers are falling short  of expectations regularly now payrolls  have ticked up slightly but less than  inflation which is an important metric  to consider so if we step back and we  say how is the Trump jobs economy it's  more or less the trendline that we saw  under Obama with some slowdown it's  basically a very similar trajectory  except it is slowing down to some degree  now how much of it is because of Trump  some but not all some of what Donald  Trump has done is already certainly  having an effect on the broader economic  conditions and then stepping back from  that there's sort of like a broader  geopolitical circumstance that exists  not in a vacuum but sort of on a  continuum and that has carried on from  the Obama presidency into the Trump  presidency now let's get to the really  important stuff because everything I've  told you so far it's important to know  but tracking these numbers will not and  does not address the real problems that  we have in the United States around  working and labor and wages I'll give  you an example here's a problem a ton of  work in our economy is assigned no  economic value and is treated as such  which has a whole number of different  effects so if you stay home to raise  kids or you take care of elderly parents  instead of working or you volunteer in  work that strengthens our communities in  ways that don't have a specifically  assigned economic value but obviously  have value to the economy if kids aren't  raised in a good way to use a really  sort of vague general term if kids  aren't raised in a way that makes them  good members of our communities that has  an economic cost they are more likely to  commit crime which costs money in terms  of police and property damage and  whatever else so you can understand how  raising kids properly has an economic  value but it's hard to exactly assign a  number to it and if you can't do that in  our economy its effect  valued at zero that's a major problem  that's not addressed by looking at did  we have a hundred and fifty thousand or  two hundred thousand jobs created issue  number two even when we look at wage  growth whether we have zero point two  percent wage growth or five percent or  we annualized it two three four or five  percent wage growth or whatever and we  look at the numbers over the last 40  years what you have to understand is  that the lion's share of the mean wage  growth that has taken place over the  last many decades has gone  disproportionately to the top almost all  of the economic gains since 1980 have  gone to the top half of earners rather  than the bottom half of earners a ton of  it has gone to the top one percent a  crazy disproportionate amount has gone  to the top 0.1% so you can argue about  Trump has 0.5 percent wage growth and  Obama had 0.4 or whatever but if most of  it goes to the rich it's not stimulating  the economy as much as it could it's  making society more unequal which  increases crime and all sorts of other  problems that needs to be solved so the  point I'm trying to make to you is that  bragging about we're up 0.2 percent in  wages or the unemployment rate has  ticked up or down 0.1 percent or 150,000  versus a hundred and sixty thousand job  creation it doesn't deal with these  long-term systemic problems that when  members of Congress are reelected every  two years when president's re-elected  every four years and senators are  reelected every six there is no sort of  a baseline incentive to actually deal  with that and by the way the political  battle over the jobs reports doesn't  touch inequality it doesn't touch work  that's valued at zero by the economy and  none of it deals with automation and  technological unemployment and the fact  that we're going to have to rethink  human relationships to work there are a  ton of ideas around how do we think  about a technological unemployment and  automation and robotics and artificial  intelligence we've heard about universal  basic income  we've heard about government-guaranteed  jobs programs negative income tax rates  the most important question is going to  be who gets the gains from technology if  nothing changes in our economic system  and we know the answer the huge  corporations and the rich people who buy  the robots and the automation technology  and the artificial intelligence  infrastructures they are going to get  the gains which is going to make the  country more unequal it's not going to  do demand-side stimulus meaning giving  people money that they can spend imagine  a company with a hundred employees and  the technology that lets them double  productivity there are lots of ways to  distribute the gains a pure capitalism  solution might be the company fires 50  people and they're able to keep  producing the same amount of stuff with  half the employees because of this great  technology it's terrible for the economy  fifty people are now out of a job they  don't have money with which they can go  and buy things from other companies so  those other companies that want to be  selling stuff to those 50 people lose  out and those other companies end up  having to let people go so that's not  good like a pure free market capitalism  solution for technological unemployment  and automation technology is not good  the employer could also say instead of  firing 50 people will keep all hundred  people but now with this new technology  that doubles productivity each employee  only has to work 20 hours a week instead  of 40 hours a week and they can all keep  making the same amount of money and the  capitalists so to speak the ownership  the business doesn't get any of the  gains now companies aren't going to do  that a fair solution would be that the  company gets a little bit of those gains  because they're buying the new  technology but the workers also get some  of it I don't know exactly what the  numbers would be but that would be sort  of a solution from the world of social  democracy but as you can see there has  to be regulation in order to prevent the  company from just taking all the gains  and firing half of their workforce and  that's just one of the problems that  needs to be solved so my message to you  today is that we can't take any  politician seriously that isn't talking  about the deeper issues job creation  numbers unemployment rates wage growth  these ideas of we're gonna bring back  manufacturing these are surface issues  that if we solve are effectively  band-aids in our economic system if a  candidate isn't talking about  restructuring the economic system  changing the relationship of humans to  work dealing with inequality in a  serious way dealing with the economic  activities that are not valued at all by  our current economy and then also by the  way dealing with activities that are  profitable for lots of people but  actually aren't economically valuable if  you look at a ton of what the financial  world's derivative instruments and  speculation do they can't actually be  taken seriously as drivers of economic  value in a long-term way a lot of that  stuff that's going on on Wall Street  merely serves to enrich people without  actually providing any stimulus to the  economy so if politicians aren't talking  about that stuff if they're talking  merely about I'll bring back of  manufacturing jobs or whatever else I  don't take them seriously and you  shouldn't take them seriously either let  me know what you think I'm on Twitter at  D Pakman the show is on Twitter at David  Pakman show
 

